INSERT INTO `strategies` VALUES (1,1,1,'```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\">\n    <title>Long-Term Care Insurance Claim Strategy - John Smith</title>\n    <style>\n        body { font-family: \'Segoe UI\', Tahoma, Geneva, Verdana, sans-serif; line-height: 1.6; color: #333; max-width: 900px; margin: 20px auto; padding: 20px; background-color: #f9f9f9; border: 1px solid #ddd; border-radius: 8px; }\n        h1, h2, h3 { color: #2c3e50; margin-top: 1.5em; }\n        h1 { border-bottom: 2px solid #3498db; padding-bottom: 10px; }\n        h2 { border-bottom: 1px solid #ccc; padding-bottom: 5px; }\n        h3 { color: #34495e; }\n        p { margin-bottom: 1em; }\n        ul { list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-bottom: 1em; }\n        ol { list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; margin-bottom: 1em; }\n        li { margin-bottom: 0.5em; }\n        strong { color: #e74c3c; }\n        .note { background-color: #ecf0f1; border-left: 5px solid #3498db; padding: 10px 15px; margin: 1em 0; border-radius: 4px; }\n        .warning { background-color: #fce4e4; border-left: 5px solid #e74c3c; padding: 10px 15px; margin: 1em 0; border-radius: 4px; }\n        table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin-bottom: 1em; }\n        th, td { border: 1px solid #ddd; padding: 8px; text-align: left; }\n        th { background-color: #f2f2f2; }\n    </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n\n    <h1>Long-Term Care Insurance Claim Strategy for John Smith</h1>\n    <p><strong>Prepared For:</strong> John Smith</p>\n    <p><strong>Date:</strong> <script>document.write(new Date().toLocaleDateString());</script></p>\n    <p>This document outlines a comprehensive strategy for initiating, managing, and optimizing your long-term care insurance claim with Genworth under policy #POL123456. As many specific policy details were not provided, this strategy incorporates best practices and placeholder guidance where necessary. It is crucial to obtain and review your specific policy document for exact terms and conditions.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"note\">\n        <strong>Important Note:</strong> This policy does NOT use Assignment of Benefits. This means you, John Smith, will be responsible for paying care providers directly and then submitting documentation to Genworth for reimbursement. This requires careful financial planning and meticulous record-keeping.\n    </div>\n\n    <h2>1. Executive Summary</h2>\n    <p>This strategy document provides a roadmap for John Smith to successfully navigate the Genworth long-term care insurance claim process. The primary goal is to ensure timely approval of benefits and consistent reimbursement for eligible care services. Given that specific policy details such as Elimination Period, Daily/Monthly/Lifetime Maximums, and ADL requirements are not yet known, the initial focus will be on gathering this critical information and preparing for the eligibility assessment. The strategy emphasizes proactive preparation, meticulous documentation, and understanding the reimbursement-based nature of your policy.</p>\n\n    <h2>2. Policy Analysis</h2>\n    <p>Understanding the specifics of your Genworth policy (Policy #POL123456) is the foundational step. Without these details, any claim initiation would be speculative. You must obtain a complete copy of your policy document if you do not have one readily available.</p>\n\n    <h3>Key Information to Verify (from your policy document):</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Elimination Period (EP):</strong> This is the number of days you must receive eligible care before benefits begin to be paid. Common periods are 0, 30, 60, 90, or 100 days. Care received during this period is not reimbursed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Daily Maximum Benefit:</strong> The maximum dollar amount Genworth will pay for eligible care in a single day.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monthly Maximum Benefit:</strong> The maximum dollar amount Genworth will pay for eligible care in a single month. This is often the Daily Maximum multiplied by 30 or 31.</li>\n        <li><strong>Lifetime Maximum Benefit:</strong> The total dollar amount Genworth will pay out over the life of the policy.</li>\n        <li><strong>Minimum ADLs Required:</strong> The number of Activities of Daily Living (ADLs) you must be unable to perform (typically 2 out of 6) or if you have severe cognitive impairment to trigger benefits.</li>\n        <li><strong>Waiver of Premium:</strong> Conditions under which you no longer have to pay premiums once benefits begin (e.g., after the Elimination Period is met).</li>\n        <li><strong>Covered Services/Care Settings:</strong> What types of care are covered (e.g., home care, assisted living, nursing home, adult day care) and any specific requirements for providers (e.g., licensed agency, certified caregiver).</li>\n        <li><strong>Cognitive Impairment Trigger:</strong> Specific definitions and criteria for eligibility based on cognitive impairment (e.g., Alzheimer\'s, dementia).</li>\n        <li><strong>Care Notes Required:</strong> Specific requirements for care provider notes (e.g., daily logs, weekly summaries).</li>\n        <li><strong>Payment Cycle:</strong> How often Genworth processes reimbursements (e.g., monthly, bi-weekly).</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <div class=\"note\">\n        <strong>Action Item:</strong> Immediately locate your original Genworth policy document (Policy #POL123456). If you cannot find it, contact Genworth directly to request a certified copy. Keep this document in a safe, accessible place.\n    </div>\n\n    <h2>3. Eligibility Assessment</h2>\n    <p>Eligibility for long-term care benefits is typically triggered by a functional or cognitive impairment. For John Smith, the assessment will focus on ADL limitations and/or cognitive impairment.</p>\n\n    <h3>ADL Limitations:</h3>\n    <p>Benefits are usually triggered when an individual is unable to perform a certain number of Activities of Daily Living (ADLs) without substantial assistance. The standard ADLs are:</p>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Bathing:</strong> Washing oneself by sponge bath or in a shower or tub, including getting into or out of the tub or shower.</li>\n        <li><strong>Dressing:</strong> Putting on and taking off all items of clothing and any necessary braces, fasteners, or artificial limbs.</li>\n        <li><strong>Toileting:</strong> Getting to and from the toilet, getting on and off the toilet, and performing associated personal hygiene.</li>\n        <li><strong>Transferring:</strong> Moving into or out of a bed, chair, or wheelchair.</li>\n        <li><strong>Continence:</strong> The ability to maintain control of bowel and bladder function; or, when unable to maintain control, the ability to perform associated personal hygiene (including caring for a catheter or colostomy bag).</li>\n        <li><strong>Eating:</strong> Performing all tasks of getting food into the body (e.g., cutting food, using utensils, getting food from plate to mouth).</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p>Your policy will specify the minimum number of ADLs (e.g., 2 out of 6) that must be impaired for benefits to trigger.</p>\n\n    <h3>Cognitive Impairment:</h3>\n    <p>If you experience severe cognitive impairment (e.g., due to Alzheimer\'s disease, dementia), this can also trigger benefits, even if you can perform all ADLs. The policy will define \"severe cognitive impairment\" and the diagnostic criteria required.</p>\n\n    <h3>Current Care Needs:</h3>\n    <p>Although not provided, it\'s essential to document any current or anticipated needs for assistance with ADLs or supervision due to cognitive decline. This will form the basis of your \"Plan of Care.\"</p>\n\n    <h2>4. Stage 1: Prior to Claim - Preparation Steps</h2>\n    <p>Proactive preparation is key to a smooth claim process. Even if care is not immediately needed, understanding the process and gathering documents is crucial.</p>\n\n    <h3>A. Gather Policy Documents:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Locate and review your Genworth policy (Policy #POL123456) in its entirety. Understand all terms, definitions, exclusions, and benefit triggers.</li>\n        <li>If you don\'t have it, contact Genworth to request a complete copy.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>B. Consult with Healthcare Professionals:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Discuss your current health status and any functional limitations with your primary care physician (PCP).</li>\n        <li>Obtain a formal diagnosis for any underlying conditions contributing to ADL limitations or cognitive impairment.</li>\n        <li>Ask your PCP to document in your medical records any difficulties you have with ADLs or cognitive function. This medical evidence is critical.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>C. Identify Potential Care Needs and Providers:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Consider what type of care you might need (e.g., home care, assisted living).</li>\n        <li>Research local licensed home care agencies or assisted living facilities that meet your policy\'s requirements.</li>\n        <li>Understand that your policy likely requires care to be provided by a licensed agency or certified individual.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>D. Establish a \"Plan of Care\":</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Before initiating a claim, a physician-approved \"Plan of Care\" is often required. This plan outlines the specific care services needed, their frequency, and who will provide them.</li>\n        <li>Work with your physician or a care manager to develop this plan. It should directly address your ADL limitations or cognitive impairment.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>E. Organize Financials and Legal Documents:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Since this is a reimbursement policy, ensure you have a system for paying care providers and tracking expenses.</li>\n        <li>Confirm your Power of Attorney (POA) is current and legally binding. Your POA, if activated, will be instrumental in managing the claim on your behalf. Ensure Genworth has a copy of the POA document on file.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h2>5. Stage 2: Filing the Claim - Step-by-Step Guide</h2>\n    <p>Once care is needed and you\'ve completed the preparation steps, you\'re ready to initiate the claim.</p>\n\n    <h3>A. Contact Genworth to Initiate Claim:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Call Genworth\'s claims department. The number should be on your policy document or their website.</li>\n        <li>Inform them you wish to initiate a long-term care insurance claim for John Smith (Policy #POL123456).</li>\n        <li>They will likely open a claim/pre-claim number for you. Note this number down.</li>\n        <li>Request the necessary claim forms. These typically include:\n            <ul>\n                <li>Claimant\'s Statement</li>\n                <li>Attending Physician\'s Statement (APS)</li>\n                <li>Care Provider\'s Statement (if applicable)</li>\n                <li>Authorization to Release Medical Information</li>\n            </ul>\n        </li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>B. Complete and Submit Claim Forms:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Claimant\'s Statement:</strong> Complete this form accurately and thoroughly. If your Power of Attorney is acting on your behalf, they will complete this.</li>\n        <li><strong>Attending Physician\'s Statement (APS):</strong> Have your primary care physician (PCP) complete this form. Ensure your PCP clearly documents your ADL limitations or cognitive impairment, linking them to your diagnosis. Provide your PCP with any relevant medical records.</li>\n        <li><strong>Care Provider\'s Statement (if applicable):</strong> If you have already engaged a care provider, they may need to complete a form detailing the services they will provide.</li>\n        <li><strong>Authorization to Release Medical Information:</strong> Sign this to allow Genworth to obtain your medical records directly from your doctors.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>C. Gather Supporting Documentation:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Medical Records:</strong> Provide copies of relevant medical records, especially those detailing the diagnosis and progression of conditions affecting your ADLs or cognitive function.</li>\n        <li><strong>Plan of Care:</strong> Submit the physician-approved Plan of Care. This is crucial for demonstrating the necessity of care.</li>\n        <li><strong>Care Provider Information:</strong> If using an agency, provide their license number and contact information. If using an individual caregiver, ensure they meet any policy requirements (e.g., certified, licensed, through an agency).</li>\n        <li><strong>Power of Attorney (if applicable):</strong> Submit a certified copy of the POA document if your POA is managing the claim.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>D. Submit All Documents:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Send all completed forms and supporting documents to Genworth via certified mail with a return receipt requested, or through their secure online portal if available.</li>\n        <li>Keep copies of everything you send for your records.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h2>6. Stage 3: Post-Approval Management - Ongoing Claim Maintenance and Reimbursement</h2>\n    <p>Once your claim is approved, the focus shifts to managing ongoing care and ensuring timely reimbursement.</p>\n\n    <h3>A. Understand Your Approval Letter:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Genworth will send an approval letter detailing the effective date of benefits, the approved daily/monthly benefit amount, the remaining Elimination Period, and any specific conditions or requirements for ongoing reimbursement.</li>\n        <li>Review this letter carefully.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>B. Meet the Elimination Period:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Continue to receive eligible care during the Elimination Period. You are responsible for paying for this care, and it will not be reimbursed.</li>\n        <li>Meticulously track these days and expenses.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>C. Pay Providers and Submit for Reimbursement:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Pay Providers Directly:</strong> As your policy does NOT use Assignment of Benefits, you must pay your care providers (e.g., home care agency, assisted living facility) directly.</li>\n        <li><strong>Obtain Detailed Invoices:</strong> Ensure all invoices from care providers are detailed. They should include:\n            <ul>\n                <li>Care recipient\'s name (John Smith)</li>\n                <li>Dates of service</li>\n                <li>Type of service provided (e.g., assistance with bathing, dressing)</li>\n                <li>Name and credentials of the caregiver</li>\n                <li>Total charges for the period</li>\n                <li>Proof of payment (e.g., \"Paid in Full\" stamp, receipt, bank statement showing payment)</li>\n            </ul>\n        </li>\n        <li><strong>Submit Reimbursement Requests:</strong> Follow Genworth\'s instructions for submitting reimbursement requests. This is typically done monthly or bi-weekly.</li>\n        <li><strong>Keep Copies:</strong> Always keep copies of all invoices, proof of payment, and submitted reimbursement forms.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>D. Ongoing Documentation:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Care Notes/Logs:</strong> Your policy likely requires care providers to maintain daily or weekly care notes documenting the specific assistance provided related to your ADL limitations or cognitive impairment. Ensure these are kept diligently.</li>\n        <li><strong>Physician Updates:</strong> Genworth may periodically request updated Attending Physician\'s Statements or medical records to confirm continued eligibility.</li>\n        <li><strong>Notify of Changes:</strong> Inform Genworth of any changes in your care needs, care providers, or living situation.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h2>7. Third-Party Evaluation Preparation</h2>\n    <p>Genworth often utilizes third-party nurses or care managers to conduct an in-person assessment to verify eligibility. This is a critical step.</p>\n\n    <h3>How to Prepare:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Review Your Condition:</strong> Be prepared to discuss your current health status, diagnoses, and how they impact your ability to perform ADLs or your cognitive function.</li>\n        <li><strong>Be Honest and Specific:</strong> Clearly articulate the specific tasks you struggle with. For example, instead of saying \"I need help bathing,\" say \"I need help getting into and out of the shower safely, and I need assistance washing my back and lower legs due to balance issues.\"</li>\n        <li><strong>Demonstrate Needs (if appropriate):</strong> If comfortable and safe, you can demonstrate the difficulty you have with certain tasks.</li>\n        <li><strong>Have Medical Records Ready:</strong> Have your medical records, especially your physician\'s notes detailing your ADL limitations or cognitive impairment, available for reference.</li>\n        <li><strong>Involve Caregivers/Family:</strong> If possible, have a family member (especially your POA) or a primary caregiver present during the assessment. They can provide valuable insights and corroborate your statements.</li>\n        <li><strong>Have Your Plan of Care:</strong> Be ready to discuss your physician-approved Plan of Care and how it addresses your needs.</li>\n        <li><strong>List Medications:</strong> Have a current list of all medications, dosages, and prescribing physicians.</li>\n        <li><strong>Ask Questions:</strong> Don\'t hesitate to ask the evaluator about the process or what they are looking for.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <div class=\"warning\">\n        <strong>Critical:</strong> Do not exaggerate your needs, but do not minimize them either. Be factual and focus on your actual functional limitations. The evaluator is looking for objective evidence.\n    </div>\n\n    <h2>8. Denial Prevention Strategy</h2>\n    <p>Avoiding common pitfalls can significantly increase your chances of a successful claim.</p>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Thorough Policy Review:</strong> Understand your policy\'s specific triggers, exclusions, and requirements before you even file.</li>\n        <li><strong>Accurate Medical Documentation:</strong> Ensure your physician\'s records clearly support your claim of ADL limitations or cognitive impairment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Comprehensive Plan of Care:</strong> A well-defined, physician-approved Plan of Care that clearly links your needs to covered services is essential.</li>\n        <li><strong>Licensed/Qualified Providers:</strong> Use only care providers (agencies or individuals) who meet your policy\'s specific requirements. Verify their credentials.</li>\n        <li><strong>Meticulous Record Keeping:</strong> Maintain detailed records of all care received, including dates, services, caregiver names, and costs. Keep copies of all invoices and proof of payment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Timely Submissions:</strong> Submit claim forms and reimbursement requests promptly.</li>\n        <li><strong>Clear Communication:</strong> Respond promptly to all requests from Genworth. Keep a log of all communications (date, time, person spoken to, summary of discussion).</li>\n        <li><strong>No Assignment of Benefits Misunderstanding:</strong> Always remember you pay first, then get reimbursed. Do not expect Genworth to pay providers directly.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h2>9. Appeal Strategy</h2>\n    <p>If your claim is denied, it\'s not the end of the road. You have the right to appeal.</p>\n\n    <h3>A. Understand the Reason for Denial:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Genworth must provide a written explanation for the denial. Review this letter carefully to understand the specific reasons.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>B. Gather Additional Evidence:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Consult with your physician to obtain further medical documentation that addresses the reasons for denial.</li>\n        <li>Obtain statements from caregivers or family members who can attest to your needs.</li>\n        <li>If the denial was due to insufficient ADL limitations, consider getting a second medical opinion or a more detailed functional assessment.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>C. Draft a Formal Appeal Letter:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Clearly state that you are appealing the denial.</li>\n        <li>Reference your policy number and claim number.</li>\n        <li>Address each reason for denial outlined in Genworth\'s letter, providing counter-arguments and supporting evidence.</li>\n        <li>Attach all new and relevant documentation.</li>\n        <li>Keep the tone professional and factual.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>D. Submit the Appeal:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Send the appeal letter and all supporting documents via certified mail with a return receipt requested.</li>\n        <li>Adhere to any deadlines specified in the denial letter for submitting an appeal.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>E. External Review:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li>If your internal appeal is denied, you may have the right to an external review by an independent third party, depending on your state\'s regulations.</li>\n        <li>Consider consulting with an elder law attorney or a long-term care insurance claims specialist at this stage.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h2>10. Important Contacts & Resources</h2>\n\n    <h3>Your Information:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Insured:</strong> John Smith</li>\n        <li><strong>DOB:</strong> 1978-02-26</li>\n        <li><strong>Policy #:</strong> POL123456</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>Genworth Financial:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Customer Service/Claims Phone:</strong> [Locate on your policy or Genworth\'s website]</li>\n        <li><strong>Claims Mailing Address:</strong> [Locate on your policy or Genworth\'s website]</li>\n        <li><strong>Website:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.genworth.com\" target=\"_blank\">www.genworth.com</a></li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>Your Healthcare Providers:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Primary Care Physician:</strong> [Name, Phone Number]</li>\n        <li><strong>Other Specialists (if applicable):</strong> [Name, Phone Number]</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>Legal/Financial Contacts (if applicable):</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Power of Attorney (POA):</strong> [Name, Contact Information]</li>\n        <li><strong>Financial Advisor:</strong> [Name, Contact Information]</li>\n        <li><strong>Elder Law Attorney:</strong> [Name, Contact Information - for potential future consultation]</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>Recommended Resources:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>National Association of Insurance Commissioners (NAIC):</strong> Provides consumer information and state insurance department contacts.</li>\n        <li><strong>Your State Department of Insurance:</strong> Can provide guidance on consumer rights and complaint processes.</li>\n        <li><strong>Long-Term Care Ombudsman Program:</strong> Advocates for residents of long-term care facilities.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <div class=\"note\">\n        This document serves as a strategic guide. It is not legal advice. Always refer to your specific Genworth policy document for exact terms, conditions, and exclusions.\n    </div>\n\n</body>\n</html>\n```','```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\">\n    <title>Long-Term Care Insurance Claim Strategy - John Smith</title>\n    <style>\n        body { font-family: \'Segoe UI\', Tahoma, Geneva, Verdana, sans-serif; line-height: 1.6; color: #333; max-width: 900px; margin: 20px auto; padding: 20px; background-color: #f9f9f9; border: 1px solid #ddd; border-radius: 8px; }\n        h1, h2, h3 { color: #2c3e50; margin-top: 1.5em; }\n        h1 { border-bottom: 2px solid #3498db; padding-bottom: 10px; }\n        h2 { border-bottom: 1px solid #ccc; padding-bottom: 5px; }\n        h3 { color: #34495e; }\n        p { margin-bottom: 1em; }\n        ul { list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-bottom: 1em; }\n        ol { list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; margin-bottom: 1em; }\n        li { margin-bottom: 0.5em; }\n        strong { color: #e74c3c; }\n        .note { background-color: #ecf0f1; border-left: 5px solid #3498db; padding: 10px 15px; margin: 1em 0; border-radius: 4px; }\n        .warning { background-color: #fce4e4; border-left: 5px solid #e74c3c; padding: 10px 15px; margin: 1em 0; border-radius: 4px; }\n        table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin-bottom: 1em; }\n        th, td { border: 1px solid #ddd; padding: 8px; text-align: left; }\n        th { background-color: #f2f2f2; }\n    </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n\n    <h1>Long-Term Care Insurance Claim Strategy for John Smith</h1>\n    <p><strong>Prepared For:</strong> John Smith</p>\n    <p><strong>Date:</strong> <script>document.write(new Date().toLocaleDateString());</script></p>\n    <p>This document outlines a comprehensive strategy for initiating, managing, and optimizing your long-term care insurance claim with Genworth under policy #POL123456. As many specific policy details were not provided, this strategy incorporates best practices and placeholder guidance where necessary. It is crucial to obtain and review your specific policy document for exact terms and conditions.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"note\">\n        <strong>Important Note:</strong> This policy does NOT use Assignment of Benefits. This means you, John Smith, will be responsible for paying care providers directly and then submitting documentation to Genworth for reimbursement. This requires careful financial planning and meticulous record-keeping.\n    </div>\n\n    <h2>1. Executive Summary</h2>\n    <p>This strategy document provides a roadmap for John Smith to successfully navigate the Genworth long-term care insurance claim process. The primary goal is to ensure timely approval of benefits and consistent reimbursement for eligible care services. Given that specific policy details such as Elimination Period, Daily/Monthly/Lifetime Maximums, and ADL requirements are not yet known, the initial focus will be on gathering this critical information and preparing for the eligibility assessment. The strategy emphasizes proactive preparation, meticulous documentation, and understanding the reimbursement-based nature of your policy.</p>\n\n    <h2>2. Policy Analysis</h2>\n    <p>Understanding the specifics of your Genworth policy (Policy #POL123456) is the foundational step. Without these details, any claim initiation would be speculative. You must obtain a complete copy of your policy document if you do not have one readily available.</p>\n\n    <h3>Key Information to Verify (from your policy document):</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Elimination Period (EP):</strong> This is the number of days you must receive eligible care before benefits begin to be paid. Common periods are 0, 30, 60, 90, or 100 days. Care received during this period is not reimbursed.</li>\n        <li><strong>Daily Maximum Benefit:</strong> The maximum dollar amount Genworth will pay for eligible care in a single day.</li>\n        <li><strong>Monthly Maximum Benefit:</strong> The maximum dollar amount Genworth will pay for eligible care in a single month. This is often the Daily Maximum multiplied by 30 or 31.</li>\n        <li><strong>Lifetime Maximum Benefit:</strong> The total dollar amount Genworth will pay out over the life of the policy.</li>\n        <li><strong>Minimum ADLs Required:</strong> The number of Activities of Daily Living (ADLs) you must be unable to perform (typically 2 out of 6) or if you have severe cognitive impairment to trigger benefits.</li>\n        <li><strong>Waiver of Premium:</strong> Conditions under which you no longer have to pay premiums once benefits begin (e.g., after the Elimination Period is met).</li>\n        <li><strong>Covered Services/Care Settings:</strong> What types of care are covered (e.g., home care, assisted living, nursing home, adult day care) and any specific requirements for providers (e.g., licensed agency, certified caregiver).</li>\n        <li><strong>Cognitive Impairment Trigger:</strong> Specific definitions and criteria for eligibility based on cognitive impairment (e.g., Alzheimer\'s, dementia).</li>\n        <li><strong>Care Notes Required:</strong> Specific requirements for care provider notes (e.g., daily logs, weekly summaries).</li>\n        <li><strong>Payment Cycle:</strong> How often Genworth processes reimbursements (e.g., monthly, bi-weekly).</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <div class=\"note\">\n        <strong>Action Item:</strong> Immediately locate your original Genworth policy document (Policy #POL123456). If you cannot find it, contact Genworth directly to request a certified copy. Keep this document in a safe, accessible place.\n    </div>\n\n    <h2>3. Eligibility Assessment</h2>\n    <p>Eligibility for long-term care benefits is typically triggered by a functional or cognitive impairment. For John Smith, the assessment will focus on ADL limitations and/or cognitive impairment.</p>\n\n    <h3>ADL Limitations:</h3>\n    <p>Benefits are usually triggered when an individual is unable to perform a certain number of Activities of Daily Living (ADLs) without substantial assistance. The standard ADLs are:</p>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Bathing:</strong> Washing oneself by sponge bath or in a shower or tub, including getting into or out of the tub or shower.</li>\n        <li><strong>Dressing:</strong> Putting on and taking off all items of clothing and any necessary braces, fasteners, or artificial limbs.</li>\n        <li><strong>Toileting:</strong> Getting to and from the toilet, getting on and off the toilet, and performing associated personal hygiene.</li>\n        <li><strong>Transferring:</strong> Moving into or out of a bed, chair, or wheelchair.</li>\n        <li><strong>Continence:</strong> The ability to maintain control of bowel and bladder function; or, when unable to maintain control, the ability to perform associated personal hygiene (including caring for a catheter or colostomy bag).</li>\n        <li><strong>Eating:</strong> Performing all tasks of getting food into the body (e.g., cutting food, using utensils, getting food from plate to mouth).</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p>Your policy will specify the minimum number of ADLs (e.g., 2 out of 6) that must be impaired for benefits to trigger.</p>\n\n    <h3>Cognitive Impairment:</h3>\n    <p>If you experience severe cognitive impairment (e.g., due to Alzheimer\'s disease, dementia), this can also trigger benefits, even if you can perform all ADLs. The policy will define \"severe cognitive impairment\" and the diagnostic criteria required.</p>\n\n    <h3>Current Care Needs:</h3>\n    <p>Although not provided, it\'s essential to document any current or anticipated needs for assistance with ADLs or supervision due to cognitive decline. This will form the basis of your \"Plan of Care.\"</p>\n\n    <h2>4. Stage 1: Prior to Claim - Preparation Steps</h2>\n    <p>Proactive preparation is key to a smooth claim process. Even if care is not immediately needed, understanding the process and gathering documents is crucial.</p>\n\n    <h3>A. Gather Policy Documents:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Locate and review your Genworth policy (Policy #POL123456) in its entirety. Understand all terms, definitions, exclusions, and benefit triggers.</li>\n        <li>If you don\'t have it, contact Genworth to request a complete copy.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>B. Consult with Healthcare Professionals:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Discuss your current health status and any functional limitations with your primary care physician (PCP).</li>\n        <li>Obtain a formal diagnosis for any underlying conditions contributing to ADL limitations or cognitive impairment.</li>\n        <li>Ask your PCP to document in your medical records any difficulties you have with ADLs or cognitive function. This medical evidence is critical.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>C. Identify Potential Care Needs and Providers:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Consider what type of care you might need (e.g., home care, assisted living).</li>\n        <li>Research local licensed home care agencies or assisted living facilities that meet your policy\'s requirements.</li>\n        <li>Understand that your policy likely requires care to be provided by a licensed agency or certified individual.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>D. Establish a \"Plan of Care\":</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Before initiating a claim, a physician-approved \"Plan of Care\" is often required. This plan outlines the specific care services needed, their frequency, and who will provide them.</li>\n        <li>Work with your physician or a care manager to develop this plan. It should directly address your ADL limitations or cognitive impairment.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>E. Organize Financials and Legal Documents:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Since this is a reimbursement policy, ensure you have a system for paying care providers and tracking expenses.</li>\n        <li>Confirm your Power of Attorney (POA) is current and legally binding. Your POA, if activated, will be instrumental in managing the claim on your behalf. Ensure Genworth has a copy of the POA document on file.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h2>5. Stage 2: Filing the Claim - Step-by-Step Guide</h2>\n    <p>Once care is needed and you\'ve completed the preparation steps, you\'re ready to initiate the claim.</p>\n\n    <h3>A. Contact Genworth to Initiate Claim:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Call Genworth\'s claims department. The number should be on your policy document or their website.</li>\n        <li>Inform them you wish to initiate a long-term care insurance claim for John Smith (Policy #POL123456).</li>\n        <li>They will likely open a claim/pre-claim number for you. Note this number down.</li>\n        <li>Request the necessary claim forms. These typically include:\n            <ul>\n                <li>Claimant\'s Statement</li>\n                <li>Attending Physician\'s Statement (APS)</li>\n                <li>Care Provider\'s Statement (if applicable)</li>\n                <li>Authorization to Release Medical Information</li>\n            </ul>\n        </li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>B. Complete and Submit Claim Forms:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Claimant\'s Statement:</strong> Complete this form accurately and thoroughly. If your Power of Attorney is acting on your behalf, they will complete this.</li>\n        <li><strong>Attending Physician\'s Statement (APS):</strong> Have your primary care physician (PCP) complete this form. Ensure your PCP clearly documents your ADL limitations or cognitive impairment, linking them to your diagnosis. Provide your PCP with any relevant medical records.</li>\n        <li><strong>Care Provider\'s Statement (if applicable):</strong> If you have already engaged a care provider, they may need to complete a form detailing the services they will provide.</li>\n        <li><strong>Authorization to Release Medical Information:</strong> Sign this to allow Genworth to obtain your medical records directly from your doctors.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>C. Gather Supporting Documentation:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Medical Records:</strong> Provide copies of relevant medical records, especially those detailing the diagnosis and progression of conditions affecting your ADLs or cognitive function.</li>\n        <li><strong>Plan of Care:</strong> Submit the physician-approved Plan of Care. This is crucial for demonstrating the necessity of care.</li>\n        <li><strong>Care Provider Information:</strong> If using an agency, provide their license number and contact information. If using an individual caregiver, ensure they meet any policy requirements (e.g., certified, licensed, through an agency).</li>\n        <li><strong>Power of Attorney (if applicable):</strong> Submit a certified copy of the POA document if your POA is managing the claim.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>D. Submit All Documents:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Send all completed forms and supporting documents to Genworth via certified mail with a return receipt requested, or through their secure online portal if available.</li>\n        <li>Keep copies of everything you send for your records.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h2>6. Stage 3: Post-Approval Management - Ongoing Claim Maintenance and Reimbursement</h2>\n    <p>Once your claim is approved, the focus shifts to managing ongoing care and ensuring timely reimbursement.</p>\n\n    <h3>A. Understand Your Approval Letter:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Genworth will send an approval letter detailing the effective date of benefits, the approved daily/monthly benefit amount, the remaining Elimination Period, and any specific conditions or requirements for ongoing reimbursement.</li>\n        <li>Review this letter carefully.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>B. Meet the Elimination Period:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Continue to receive eligible care during the Elimination Period. You are responsible for paying for this care, and it will not be reimbursed.</li>\n        <li>Meticulously track these days and expenses.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>C. Pay Providers and Submit for Reimbursement:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Pay Providers Directly:</strong> As your policy does NOT use Assignment of Benefits, you must pay your care providers (e.g., home care agency, assisted living facility) directly.</li>\n        <li><strong>Obtain Detailed Invoices:</strong> Ensure all invoices from care providers are detailed. They should include:\n            <ul>\n                <li>Care recipient\'s name (John Smith)</li>\n                <li>Dates of service</li>\n                <li>Type of service provided (e.g., assistance with bathing, dressing)</li>\n                <li>Name and credentials of the caregiver</li>\n                <li>Total charges for the period</li>\n                <li>Proof of payment (e.g., \"Paid in Full\" stamp, receipt, bank statement showing payment)</li>\n            </ul>\n        </li>\n        <li><strong>Submit Reimbursement Requests:</strong> Follow Genworth\'s instructions for submitting reimbursement requests. This is typically done monthly or bi-weekly.</li>\n        <li><strong>Keep Copies:</strong> Always keep copies of all invoices, proof of payment, and submitted reimbursement forms.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>D. Ongoing Documentation:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Care Notes/Logs:</strong> Your policy likely requires care providers to maintain daily or weekly care notes documenting the specific assistance provided related to your ADL limitations or cognitive impairment. Ensure these are kept diligently.</li>\n        <li><strong>Physician Updates:</strong> Genworth may periodically request updated Attending Physician\'s Statements or medical records to confirm continued eligibility.</li>\n        <li><strong>Notify of Changes:</strong> Inform Genworth of any changes in your care needs, care providers, or living situation.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h2>7. Third-Party Evaluation Preparation</h2>\n    <p>Genworth often utilizes third-party nurses or care managers to conduct an in-person assessment to verify eligibility. This is a critical step.</p>\n\n    <h3>How to Prepare:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Review Your Condition:</strong> Be prepared to discuss your current health status, diagnoses, and how they impact your ability to perform ADLs or your cognitive function.</li>\n        <li><strong>Be Honest and Specific:</strong> Clearly articulate the specific tasks you struggle with. For example, instead of saying \"I need help bathing,\" say \"I need help getting into and out of the shower safely, and I need assistance washing my back and lower legs due to balance issues.\"</li>\n        <li><strong>Demonstrate Needs (if appropriate):</strong> If comfortable and safe, you can demonstrate the difficulty you have with certain tasks.</li>\n        <li><strong>Have Medical Records Ready:</strong> Have your medical records, especially your physician\'s notes detailing your ADL limitations or cognitive impairment, available for reference.</li>\n        <li><strong>Involve Caregivers/Family:</strong> If possible, have a family member (especially your POA) or a primary caregiver present during the assessment. They can provide valuable insights and corroborate your statements.</li>\n        <li><strong>Have Your Plan of Care:</strong> Be ready to discuss your physician-approved Plan of Care and how it addresses your needs.</li>\n        <li><strong>List Medications:</strong> Have a current list of all medications, dosages, and prescribing physicians.</li>\n        <li><strong>Ask Questions:</strong> Don\'t hesitate to ask the evaluator about the process or what they are looking for.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <div class=\"warning\">\n        <strong>Critical:</strong> Do not exaggerate your needs, but do not minimize them either. Be factual and focus on your actual functional limitations. The evaluator is looking for objective evidence.\n    </div>\n\n    <h2>8. Denial Prevention Strategy</h2>\n    <p>Avoiding common pitfalls can significantly increase your chances of a successful claim.</p>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Thorough Policy Review:</strong> Understand your policy\'s specific triggers, exclusions, and requirements before you even file.</li>\n        <li><strong>Accurate Medical Documentation:</strong> Ensure your physician\'s records clearly support your claim of ADL limitations or cognitive impairment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Comprehensive Plan of Care:</strong> A well-defined, physician-approved Plan of Care that clearly links your needs to covered services is essential.</li>\n        <li><strong>Licensed/Qualified Providers:</strong> Use only care providers (agencies or individuals) who meet your policy\'s specific requirements. Verify their credentials.</li>\n        <li><strong>Meticulous Record Keeping:</strong> Maintain detailed records of all care received, including dates, services, caregiver names, and costs. Keep copies of all invoices and proof of payment.</li>\n        <li><strong>Timely Submissions:</strong> Submit claim forms and reimbursement requests promptly.</li>\n        <li><strong>Clear Communication:</strong> Respond promptly to all requests from Genworth. Keep a log of all communications (date, time, person spoken to, summary of discussion).</li>\n        <li><strong>No Assignment of Benefits Misunderstanding:</strong> Always remember you pay first, then get reimbursed. Do not expect Genworth to pay providers directly.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h2>9. Appeal Strategy</h2>\n    <p>If your claim is denied, it\'s not the end of the road. You have the right to appeal.</p>\n\n    <h3>A. Understand the Reason for Denial:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Genworth must provide a written explanation for the denial. Review this letter carefully to understand the specific reasons.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>B. Gather Additional Evidence:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Consult with your physician to obtain further medical documentation that addresses the reasons for denial.</li>\n        <li>Obtain statements from caregivers or family members who can attest to your needs.</li>\n        <li>If the denial was due to insufficient ADL limitations, consider getting a second medical opinion or a more detailed functional assessment.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>C. Draft a Formal Appeal Letter:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Clearly state that you are appealing the denial.</li>\n        <li>Reference your policy number and claim number.</li>\n        <li>Address each reason for denial outlined in Genworth\'s letter, providing counter-arguments and supporting evidence.</li>\n        <li>Attach all new and relevant documentation.</li>\n        <li>Keep the tone professional and factual.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>D. Submit the Appeal:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Send the appeal letter and all supporting documents via certified mail with a return receipt requested.</li>\n        <li>Adhere to any deadlines specified in the denial letter for submitting an appeal.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>E. External Review:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li>If your internal appeal is denied, you may have the right to an external review by an independent third party, depending on your state\'s regulations.</li>\n        <li>Consider consulting with an elder law attorney or a long-term care insurance claims specialist at this stage.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h2>10. Important Contacts & Resources</h2>\n\n    <h3>Your Information:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Insured:</strong> John Smith</li>\n        <li><strong>DOB:</strong> 1978-02-26</li>\n        <li><strong>Policy #:</strong> POL123456</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>Genworth Financial:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Customer Service/Claims Phone:</strong> [Locate on your policy or Genworth\'s website]</li>\n        <li><strong>Claims Mailing Address:</strong> [Locate on your policy or Genworth\'s website]</li>\n        <li><strong>Website:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.genworth.com\" target=\"_blank\">www.genworth.com</a></li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>Your Healthcare Providers:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Primary Care Physician:</strong> [Name, Phone Number]</li>\n        <li><strong>Other Specialists (if applicable):</strong> [Name, Phone Number]</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>Legal/Financial Contacts (if applicable):</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Power of Attorney (POA):</strong> [Name, Contact Information]</li>\n        <li><strong>Financial Advisor:</strong> [Name, Contact Information]</li>\n        <li><strong>Elder Law Attorney:</strong> [Name, Contact Information - for potential future consultation]</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>Recommended Resources:</h3>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>National Association of Insurance Commissioners (NAIC):</strong> Provides consumer information and state insurance department contacts.</li>\n        <li><strong>Your State Department of Insurance:</strong> Can provide guidance on consumer rights and complaint processes.</li>\n        <li><strong>Long-Term Care Ombudsman Program:</strong> Advocates for residents of long-term care facilities.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <div class=\"note\">\n        This document serves as a strategic guide. It is not legal advice. Always refer to your specific Genworth policy document for exact terms, conditions, and exclusions.\n    </div>\n\n</body>\n</html>\n```','2026-02-27 16:50:23','2026-02-27 16:50:23','2026-04-21 06:16:43',1,'[{\"category\": \"carrier-contact\", \"completed\": true, \"id\": \"s1-1\", \"label\": \"Contact insurance company and request claims packet by mail\"}, {\"category\": \"carrier-contact\", \"completed\": true, \"critical\": true, \"id\": \"s1-2\", \"label\": \"DO NOT initiate the claim yet — only request information\"}, {\"category\": \"carrier-contact\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s1-3\", \"label\": \"Request Assignment of Benefits (AOB) form from carrier\"}, {\"category\": \"medical-evaluation\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s1-4\", \"label\": \"Evaluate long-term care need and identify physical/cognitive decline\"}, {\"category\": \"medical-evaluation\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s1-5\", \"label\": \"Identify ADL deficits (minimum 2 ADLs required)\"}, {\"category\": \"provider-setup\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s1-6\", \"label\": \"Identify all healthcare providers (PCP, specialists, facilities)\"}, {\"category\": \"provider-setup\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s1-7\", \"label\": \"Notify all providers IN WRITING of intent to file LTC claim\"}, {\"category\": \"provider-setup\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s1-8\", \"label\": \"Request documented decline records from providers\"}, {\"category\": \"provider-setup\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s1-9\", \"label\": \"Schedule visit with PCP to document current condition\"}, {\"category\": \"provider-setup\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s1-10\", \"label\": \"Request chart documentation from all providers\"}, {\"category\": \"provider-setup\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s1-11\", \"label\": \"Secure home care provider with LTC insurance experience\"}, {\"category\": \"provider-setup\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s1-12\", \"label\": \"Confirm provider is familiar with LTC insurance requirements\"}, {\"category\": \"documents\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s1-13\", \"label\": \"Collect provider license\", \"uploadRequired\": true}, {\"category\": \"documents\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s1-14\", \"label\": \"Obtain Plan of Care from provider\", \"uploadRequired\": true}, {\"category\": \"documents\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s1-15\", \"label\": \"Collect provider W-9\", \"uploadRequired\": true}, {\"category\": \"documents\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s1-16\", \"label\": \"Ensure invoicing and daily care log system is in place\"}, {\"category\": \"elimination-period\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s1-17\", \"label\": \"Begin services to trigger elimination period (check policy)\"}, {\"category\": \"personal-info\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s1-18\", \"label\": \"Gather insured\'s personal identifiers (SSN, Medicare/Medicaid #)\"}, {\"category\": \"personal-info\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s1-19\", \"label\": \"Obtain Power of Attorney documentation (if applicable)\", \"uploadRequired\": true}, {\"category\": \"documents\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s1-20\", \"label\": \"Collect insurance declaration page\", \"uploadRequired\": true}, {\"category\": \"documents\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s1-21\", \"label\": \"Gather discharge paperwork (if applicable)\", \"uploadRequired\": true}]','[{\"category\": \"claim-packet\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s2-1\", \"label\": \"Complete the claim packet thoroughly\"}, {\"category\": \"claim-packet\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s2-2\", \"label\": \"Create document index listing all attachments\"}, {\"category\": \"claim-packet\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s2-3\", \"label\": \"Attach Power of Attorney (POA)\", \"uploadRequired\": true}, {\"category\": \"claim-packet\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s2-4\", \"label\": \"Attach privacy authorization form\", \"uploadRequired\": true}, {\"category\": \"claim-packet\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s2-6\", \"label\": \"Attach provider license\", \"uploadRequired\": true}, {\"category\": \"claim-packet\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s2-7\", \"label\": \"Attach insurance declaration page\", \"uploadRequired\": true}, {\"category\": \"claim-packet\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s2-8\", \"label\": \"Attach Plan of Care\", \"uploadRequired\": true}, {\"category\": \"claim-packet\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s2-9\", \"label\": \"Attach all Stage 1 supporting documentation\"}, {\"category\": \"verification\", \"completed\": false, \"critical\": true, \"id\": \"s2-10\", \"label\": \"VERIFY: Policy number appears on EVERY page\"}, {\"category\": \"submission\", \"completed\": false, \"critical\": true, \"id\": \"s2-11\", \"label\": \"SUBMIT: Send ALL documents in ONE complete packet\"}, {\"category\": \"post-submission\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s2-17\", \"label\": \"Note: Expect 30-day review period after submission\"}, {\"category\": \"post-submission\", \"completed\": false, \"critical\": true, \"id\": \"s2-18\", \"label\": \"Understand: Appeals have low success probability — get it right the first time\"}]','[{\"category\": \"ongoing\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s3-1\", \"label\": \"Continue paying for services during elimination period\"}, {\"category\": \"ongoing\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s3-2\", \"label\": \"Organize and retain all paid invoices\"}, {\"category\": \"ongoing\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s3-3\", \"label\": \"Confirm care notes document minimum ADL assistance (2+ ADLs)\"}, {\"category\": \"submission\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s3-4\", \"label\": \"Submit invoices and care logs per payment cycle (check policy)\"}, {\"category\": \"submission\", \"completed\": false, \"critical\": true, \"id\": \"s3-5\", \"label\": \"Ensure policy number appears on every submitted page\"}, {\"category\": \"reimbursement-flow\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s3-6\", \"label\": \"Pay provider directly for services\"}, {\"category\": \"reimbursement-flow\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s3-7\", \"label\": \"Submit paid invoices and documentation to insurance company\"}, {\"category\": \"reimbursement-flow\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s3-8\", \"label\": \"Track reimbursement payments from insurance company\"}, {\"category\": \"ongoing\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s3-9\", \"label\": \"Maintain ongoing communication with case manager\"}, {\"category\": \"ongoing\", \"completed\": false, \"id\": \"s3-10\", \"label\": \"Keep all records organized for potential audits\"}]',NULL,'not_started');
